; =====================================================================
; dcbor-profile.derived.cddl
;
; DERIVED - not verbatim.  draft-mcnally-deterministic-cbor-18 ships no
; CDDL profile of dCBOR.  The one CDDL rule it contains is a quotation of
; the RFC 8610 Standard Prelude, held in cddl-prelude-number.cddl; every
; rule below was written for this record from the source's prose and
; tables, and must never be quoted as the source's own grammar.
;
; Derived from draft-mcnally-deterministic-cbor-18:
;   Section 2      the recursion rule: a data item conforms only if every
;                  item nested within it conforms, "including array
;                  elements, map keys and values, and the contents of
;                  tagged data items"
;   Section 2.5    DCBOR_INT = [-2^63, 2^64-1]; Numeric Reduction; the
;                  dCBOR numeric model (untagged major types 0, 1, 7
;                  only; tagged items are outside it)
;   Section 2.6    simple values: only false, true, null, and floats
;   Section 3      tag 201, and the .dcbor / .dcborseq control operators
;   Section 6      Table 1 (tag 201 registration), Table 2 (operators)
;   Section 7.2    Table 4, rows 3 and 4: a 65-bit negative integer MUST
;                  be rejected -- the only statement anywhere in the
;                  document that bounds *integers* by DCBOR_INT
;   Section 8.5    (non-normative) the tag escape hatch: bignums, and
;                  tags 80/81/82 for nontrivial NaNs
;
; The language is CDDL (RFC 8610).  No prelude name is defined or
; redefined here: every rule carries a `dcbor-` prefix, so this file can
; be compiled against the prelude (which cddl-prelude-number.cddl cannot).
;
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; SCOPE, and why this file is weak on purpose.
;
; CDDL describes instances of the CBOR *generic data model*.  dCBOR is
; almost entirely a narrowing of the *encoding*: definite length only,
; preferred serialization, bytewise map key order, one NaN, NFC.  None of
; those are properties of a data model instance, so no CDDL can state
; them.  This is not a limitation of this file's drafting -- it is why
; Section 3 of the source adds the control operators .dcbor and .dcborseq
; instead of publishing a CDDL profile: the operators delegate the check
; to a dCBOR implementation precisely because CDDL cannot perform it.
;
; What this file DOES decide, and nothing else in CDDL can:
;   * major type 7 is restricted to false, true, null and the floats,
;     so `undefined` and simple values 0-19 and 32-255 are excluded
;     (Section 2.6) -- the one dCBOR rule that is purely data-model level;
;   * integers are bounded to DCBOR_INT, narrowing the prelude's `nint`,
;     whose lower bound is -2^64 (from Table 4; see note below);
;   * the recursion of Section 2: tag content, array elements, and both
;     halves of every map entry are themselves dcbor-data-item.
;
; What it does NOT decide is listed in full at the foot of this file and
; in README.md.  A fixture can satisfy every rule below and still not be
; dCBOR.  A green result here is NOT a conformance result.  The decision
; procedure is dcbor-validate.derived.pseudocode.txt, which works on
; encoded bytes; use this file for the shape of application data, and
; that one to decide conformance.
;
; ROOT.  RFC 8610 takes the first rule as the root, so `dcbor-data-item`
; is the default: any dCBOR-conforming data item, with any tag number.
; =====================================================================

dcbor-data-item = dcbor-number
                / dcbor-bstr
                / dcbor-tstr
                / dcbor-array
                / dcbor-map
                / dcbor-tagged
                / dcbor-simple

; --- numbers ---------------------------------------------------------
; Section 2.5.  The dCBOR numeric model is untagged major types 0, 1 and
; 7 only.  `dcbor-number` is this record's narrowing of the prelude's
; `number` (= int / float), which Section 3 recommends dCBOR applications
; use for numeric fields; it is a separate name because redefining a
; prelude rule is an error.

dcbor-number = dcbor-int / dcbor-float

; DCBOR_INT, exactly as Section 2.5 writes it: [-2^63, 2^64-1].  The
; prelude's `int` is wider on the negative side (`nint` reaches -2^64),
; and Table 4 rejects 3b8000000000000000 (-2^63 - 1) and
; 3bffffffffffffffff (-2^64) as "65-bit negative integer value".
dcbor-int = -9223372036854775808..18446744073709551615

; GAP (see item 1 below): this admits integral float values, which
; Numeric Reduction forbids.  It cannot be narrowed in CDDL.
dcbor-float = float

; --- strings ---------------------------------------------------------
; GAP: `tstr` cannot require NFC (Section 2.7), and neither string rule
; can require definite length (Section 2.1) -- Section 2 of RFC 8949
; makes the serialization variant invisible at this level.
dcbor-bstr = bstr
dcbor-tstr = tstr

; --- containers ------------------------------------------------------
; Section 2: elements, keys and values are themselves dCBOR items.
dcbor-array = [* dcbor-data-item]

; GAP: CDDL maps are unordered and CDDL cannot require distinct keys, so
; neither Section 2.3 (bytewise lexicographic order) nor Section 2.4 (no
; duplicate keys) is expressible -- and Section 2.5's warning that
; Numeric Reduction can *create* duplicate keys (Figures 1 and 2) is
; therefore invisible here too.
dcbor-map = {* dcbor-data-item => dcbor-data-item}

; --- tags ------------------------------------------------------------
; Section 2 requires the tag *content* to conform; the source places no
; restriction on which tag numbers may appear, and Section 8.5 is
; explicit that tags are the escape hatch for data items dCBOR does not
; cover.  So any tag number, with conforming content.
dcbor-tagged = #6(dcbor-data-item)

; Tag 201 (Section 3, Section 6 Table 1): "enclosed dCBOR", data item
; "(any)".  Subsumed by dcbor-tagged above; named here so a fixture that
; is specifically a tag-201 item can be validated against this rule as a
; root.  Note Section 2.5: a tagged item is never numerically equal to an
; untagged one, so wrapping in 201 changes the item's identity.
dcbor-tag-201 = #6.201(dcbor-data-item)

; --- simple values ----------------------------------------------------
; Section 2.6, the only dCBOR rule that CDDL fully enforces: `undefined`
; (Section 8.2) and every other simple value (Section 8.4) are excluded
; by leaving them out.
dcbor-simple = false / true / null

; =====================================================================
; INFORMATIVE -- from Section 8.5, which the source marks non-normative.
; These rules are not dCBOR requirements.  They record the escape hatch
; Section 8.5 names: dCBOR "places no restrictions beyond those that
; apply to all byte strings (definite length only)" on bignum content,
; and an application needing a nontrivial NaN is directed to tags 80, 81
; and 82 rather than to a float.  Both are already admitted by
; dcbor-tagged; they are spelled out so a reviewer can see that dCBOR's
; single-NaN rule (Section 2.5) has a documented bypass at the tag level.
dcbor-bignum-positive = #6.2(dcbor-bstr)
dcbor-bignum-negative = #6.3(dcbor-bstr)
dcbor-float-array-tags = #6.80(dcbor-bstr) / #6.81(dcbor-bstr)
                       / #6.82(dcbor-bstr)

; =====================================================================
; THE CONTROL OPERATORS of Section 3 are given in prose only: ".dcbor and
; .dcborseq are exactly like .cbor and .cborseq as defined in [RFC8610]
; except that they also require the encoded data item(s) to conform to
; dCBOR."  RFC 8610 Section 3.8.4 defines `.cbor` on a byte string, so
; the usage below follows; it is commented out because no CDDL tool
; implements an operator that Section 6 only requests IANA register, and
; an unknown control operator is a parse error.
;
;   dcbor-embedded     = bstr .dcbor    dcbor-data-item
;   dcbor-embedded-seq = bstr .dcborseq [* dcbor-data-item]
;
; Where a tool does implement them, `.dcbor` is strictly stronger than
; anything in this file: it reaches the encoded bytes, and therefore the
; nine gaps below.
;
; =====================================================================
; WHAT THIS CDDL CANNOT EXPRESS.  Stated gaps, not omissions; each is a
; real dCBOR requirement, each lives in ../requirements.yaml, and each is
; decided by dcbor-validate.derived.pseudocode.txt instead.
;
;  1. NUMERIC REDUCTION (Section 2.5, rule 1) - the largest gap, and the
;     heart of dCBOR.  `dcbor-float` above admits 42.0, -0.0 and 12.0,
;     every one of which a dCBOR encoder MUST emit as an integer and a
;     dCBOR decoder MUST reject as a float (Table 4 rejects f94a00 for
;     exactly this).  CDDL has no operator for "has a non-zero fractional
;     part": `.ne` compares values, and a float and the integer it
;     reduces to are *numerically equal*, so no `.ne` formulation
;     excludes them.  See numeric-reduction.derived.pseudocode.txt.
;  2. THE SINGLE NaN (Section 2.5, rule 2).  All NaNs MUST reduce to
;     0xf97e00.  IEEE 754 makes every NaN compare unequal to every value
;     including itself (the source's own observation, Section 8.3), so no
;     CDDL value comparison can pin one NaN and exclude the rest.
;  3. PREFERRED SERIALIZATION (Section 2.2).  "Shortest encoding that
;     preserves the value" is a predicate over the encoding, which the
;     generic data model deliberately hides.  Table 4 rejects
;     fb3ff8000000000000 for 1.5 and fa7f800000 for Infinity; nothing
;     here can tell them from f93e00 and f97c00.
;  4. DEFINITE LENGTH ONLY (Section 2.1).  Same reason: `[1, 2]` encoded
;     0x820102 and the same item encoded 0x9f0102ff are one instance of
;     the data model.  dcbor-head.derived.abnf decides this one.
;  5. BYTEWISE LEXICOGRAPHIC MAP KEY ORDER (Section 2.3).  CDDL maps are
;     unordered.
;  6. NO DUPLICATE MAP KEYS (Section 2.4).  CDDL cannot require
;     distinctness across a `* keytype => valuetype` entry -- and under
;     Numeric Reduction the duplicate may not even be visible in the
;     application's own data (Section 2.5, Figures 1 and 2: the keys 10
;     and 10.0 are distinct before encoding and identical after).
;  7. NFC (Section 2.7).  `tstr` constrains the major type only.  No
;     CDDL operator applies a Unicode normalization form.
;  8. "A dCBOR DECODER MUST REJECT ..." (Sections 2.1-2.7, decoder rules
;     throughout).  Non-derivability is not rejection: an implementation
;     that silently repairs a violation produces data this schema
;     accepts while failing the MUST.  Only a differential test sees it.
;  9. THE DECLARATIVE FORCE OF TAG 201 (Section 3).  `dcbor-tag-201`
;     above checks the shape of the content; the tag's meaning is an
;     assertion that the content conforms at "the encoded data item
;     level" too, which is gaps 1-8 again.
;
; Consequence, worth stating plainly: of dCBOR's seven narrowing rules,
; this CDDL enforces one and a half -- Section 2.6 in full and the
; DCBOR_INT bound of Section 2.5 -- and cannot see the other five and a
; half.  Any harness built on this directory should report it that way.
; =====================================================================
